    {
      "id": "AUD-013-BLD001",
      "severity": "high",
      "title": "Deriver artifact verifier lists UI labels as required element ids; pnpm verify and Docker fail",
      "file": "apps/key-derivation/scripts/verify-key-derivation-artifact.mjs",
      "lines": "134-135",
      "legacyId": "H-01",
      "category": "BLD",
      "kind": "finding",
      "status": "open",
      "releaseBlocking": null,
      "detail": "- **File:** [apps/key-derivation/scripts/verify-key-derivation-artifact.mjs:134-135](../../apps/key-derivation/scripts/verify-key-derivation-artifact.mjs#L134) — the required-id list contains `'Mnemonic construction details'` and `'BIP39 entropy · hexadecimal'`. Both are UI strings (`view.ts:382` sets them as `<summary>` text at runtime); the same strings correctly appear in the *required text* list at lines 247–248.\n- **Observed:** local `VERIFICATION_DIRTY=false pnpm verify` → `Error: Standalone artifact is missing required element #Mnemonic construction details.` (exit 1, after all builds succeeded). Uncached `docker build --target artifacts` for `6bbff3c` fails at `Dockerfile.reproducible` `RUN --network=none pnpm verify` with the same message (10:46 UTC).\n- **Impact:** no canonical artifacts, `verification-record.json`, CI green, or release from this commit. The artifacts themselves are fine (16/16 and 42/42 browser cases pass on them).\n- **Fix:** remove the two strings from the id list (they remain in the text list); add a unit test that every entry of the id list matches `/^[a-z0-9-]+$/`.",
      "evidence": {
        "recordedIn": "audit-13-2026-09-17.md",
        "note": "See preserved reproduction and verification sections; no new checks were executed during normalization."
      },
      "reproduction": null,
      "expected": null,
      "observed": null,
      "impact": null,
      "recommendedFix": null,
      "requiredVerification": null
    },
    {
      "id": "AUD-013-BLD002",
      "severity": "medium",
      "title": "Discovery Scanner builds with seed-discovery but without custom-paths crash at startup (features.describeCustomPath!)",
      "file": "apps/discovery-scanner/src/view.ts",
      "lines": "378-385",
      "legacyId": "M-01",
      "category": "BLD",
      "kind": "finding",
      "status": "open",
      "releaseBlocking": null,
      "detail": "- **Files:** [apps/discovery-scanner/src/view.ts:378-385](../../apps/discovery-scanner/src/view.ts#L378) (`features.describeCustomPath!(…)` inside the path-refresh routine executed during view initialisation), [apps/discovery-scanner/src/feature-selection.ts](../../apps/discovery-scanner/src/feature-selection.ts) (optional-function bag), [tooling/discovery-composition.mjs:257-264](../../tooling/discovery-composition.mjs#L257) (omits the functions when the feature is excluded).\n- **Observed:** `--tool discovery-scanner --coins dash --features seed-discovery`, `--coins bitcoin,dash,ethereum --features seed-discovery` and `--coins dash --features seed-discovery,watch-only-discovery` all build, but Chromium raises `TypeError: n.describeCustomPath is not a function` and the vault self-test never passes. Adding `custom-paths` or building watch-only-only works.\n- **Why tests missed it:** the 66-variant smoke matrix compiles without loading; `verify-selective-browser-smoke.mjs` loads only Activity Viewer variants. `docs/BUILD_MODULES.md` lists `custom-paths` as an independent optional feature, so this composition is documented as valid.\n- **Fix:** guard the custom-path refresh with `features.customPaths` (or move the custom-path controls into their own installer, see §5), and extend the selective browser smoke to one Scanner variant per feature subset and one Deriver variant per feature.",
      "evidence": {
        "recordedIn": "audit-13-2026-09-17.md",
        "note": "See preserved reproduction and verification sections; no new checks were executed during normalization."
      },
      "reproduction": null,
      "expected": null,
      "observed": null,
      "impact": null,
      "recommendedFix": null,
      "requiredVerification": null
    },
    {
      "id": "AUD-013-ARC001",
      "severity": "medium",
      "title": "Dead entrypoints kept by allow-list strings: network-worker.ts, network-worker-dash-community.ts, secret-vault/vault-protocol.ts",
      "legacyId": "M-02",
      "category": "ARC",
      "kind": "finding",
      "status": "open",
      "releaseBlocking": null,
      "detail": "- `apps/discovery-scanner/src/network-worker.ts` and `apps/discovery-scanner/src/network-worker-dash-community.ts`: since `f064ce3` the Network Worker source is generated in memory (`discoveryNetworkWorkerEntry`, esbuild `stdin`), so neither file is imported or bundled. They are referenced only by `build-discovery-scanner-html.mjs:166` (`input.endsWith('/network-worker.ts')`) and `verify-discovery-scanner-artifact.mjs:399`, i.e. the graph allow-list names inputs that can never occur. The Dash file still hard-wires `DirectRecoveryNetworkService` with the full 14-operation validator, contradicting the generated-allowlist design if anyone ever wired it back.\n- `packages/secret-vault/src/vault-protocol.ts`: `DEFAULT_VAULT_RUNTIME_OPTIONS` has no consumer; the file is referenced only as a string in `build-profiles.mjs`. The package's `tests/` directory is empty after `createVaultChannel` was removed.\n- **Fix:** delete the three files, drop the corresponding allow-list entries, and make the graph allow-lists fail if an entry names a file that does not exist.",
      "evidence": {
        "recordedIn": "audit-13-2026-09-17.md",
        "note": "See preserved reproduction and verification sections; no new checks were executed during normalization."
      },
      "reproduction": null,
